    "narrative": "The primary engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, who commands an elite score of 82.38 against the struggling arm of G. Holmes. While the model accounted for a 4% efficiency hit due to Holmes\u2019s ability to control the run game, **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier asset given his potential for category-sweeping impact. He is flanked by a high-upside pairing in the outfield; **Mickey Moniak** (69.13) benefits significantly from a favorable wind-out condition and a strong platoon split against L. Webb, while **Seiya Suzuki** (68.79) is positioned perfectly in the two-hole to capitalize on a favorable matchup against McGreevy.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved navigating the bench, where **Jose Caballero** presented a compelling case with a base projection of 59.45. Despite a massive speed edge against Fisher\u2014a target we covet for our scarcity-driven strategy\u2014the optimizer ultimately kept **Jose Caballero** on the bench due to his absence from the confirmed starting lineup. We are also closely monitoring the health of **James Wood** and **Trevor Story**, who remain sidelined on the IL, limiting our depth as we chase production from **Austin Riley** (60.98), whose start is precarious despite the favorable xERA of E. Rodriguez, largely dragged down by an unfavorable eight-hole assignment.\n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding **Travis Bazzana** (45.07), who faces a difficult same-handed matchup against W. Peralta. His scoring is suppressed by a 9% platoon penalty, making him the most likely candidate to be swapped if a mid-game defensive replacement or lineup shift occurs. While we await official word on the status of depth pieces like **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Tyler Soderstrom**, the current configuration maximizes our daily efficiency, though we remain vulnerable to volatility from our lower-order bats like **Max Muncy** (57.51) and **Cole Young** (51.18).",
